BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Susan Stryker&#x27\;s Toronto BOOK LAUNCH for Changing Gender
DTSTART:20261006T193000
DTEND:20261006T193000
UID:a6c5c4f75d4b1d8a392b16ce8f2f024f@yohomo.ca
DESCRIPTION:In a very rare Toronto appearance\, legendary trans trailblaze
 r SUSAN STRYKER will join us at Glad Day to launch her new book. It&#x27\;
 s the TORONTO BOOK LAUNCH for Susan Stryker&#x27\;s CHANGING GENDER Schedu
 le: 7pm - Doors 7:30pm - Introduction by Sheila Cavanagh 7:40pm - Presenta
 tion on the Legacy of Susan Stryker by Toby Anne Finlay 7:50pm - Interview
  with Susan Stryker and Sheila Cavanagh 8:30pm - Questions and Answers wit
 h the Audience 9:00pm - Book Signing and Social RSVP This event is FREE to
  attend. We strongly encourage you to RSVP to attend\, which helps us with
  our planning. This event is made possible\, in part\, by a SSHRC research
  grant held by Dr. Sheila Cavanagh at York University. Changing Gender is 
 a provocative\, genre-bending call to reconsider the story we tell about g
 ender itself. With the far right weaponizing the rhetoric of anti-gender i
 deology and increasingly targeting the lives of transgender people\, one q
 uestion becomes ever more urgent and contested: What are we even talking a
 bout when we talk about gender? Where did the concept itself come from\, a
 nd where might it go? In Changing Gender\, the leading trans scholar Susan
  Stryker invites readers to join her lifelong quest to uncover what gender
  means and does. She traces the concept’s roots in grammar and shows how
  it has been transformed into a battleground in the culture wars. From the
  origins of “Yankee Doodle” to acid trips in Joshua Tree National Park
 \, from nineteenth-century phrenology to present-day anti-trans conspiracy
  theorists\, Stryker finds surprising places to tap into the origins\, idi
 osyncrasies\, and generative possibilities of the gender concept. Along th
 e way\, she weaves in stories drawn from her lifetime as a pathbreaking hi
 storian\, filmmaker\, activist\, and founding figure of transgender studie
 s. Poignant and deeply researched\, lyrical and authoritative\, Changing G
 ender exposes the limiting assumptions about gender that span the politica
 l spectrum and imagines what life might look like if some of those limitat
 ions were lifted. Ultimately\, Stryker argues\, to change gender is to cha
 nge what it means to be human—and to change reality itself. Published by
  Farrar\, Straus and Giroux. Susan Stryker’s historical research\, theor
 etical writings\, media-making\, activism\, and academic field-building ac
 tivities have helped shape the conversation on trans issues since the earl
 y 1990s. Susan retired from the University of Arizona as Professor of Gend
 er and Women&#x27\;s Studies in 2020\, and currently holds a distinguished
  visiting appointment at Stanford University&#x27\;s Michelle Clayman Inst
 itute for Gender Research. Related forthcoming Special Issues of Australia
 n Feminist Studies: one on Susan Stryker’s legacy and a second on trans 
 studies more broadly ‍
LOCATION:Glad Day (32 Lisgar)\, 32 Lisgar
URL:https://www.yohomo.ca/event/susan-strykers-toronto-book-launch-for-cha
 nging-gender
X-SOURCE:YOHOMO
END:VEVENT
